course: dictionary
lesson: 5814
title: Magnetic: pulling and describing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Magnetic means able to pull iron or steel towards itself, like a magnet. Iron nails are magnetic, but wood and plastic are not.
explain: The word can also describe things linked to magnets, like the «magnetic field» around the Earth. Read the words near it: «field» and «poles» tell you it is about magnetism, not about a person.
explain: Sometimes the word describes a person. «She was a magnetic speaker.» Here the clue is that people are strongly drawn to her, the way a magnet pulls metal. Always check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence.
fact: Magnetic means able to pull iron or steel towards itself.
fact: Iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are magnetic; wood, plastic, glass and paper are not.
fact: A compass needle is a small magnet free to turn, and it lines up with the Earth's magnetic field.
fact: «Magnetic north» and «magnetic poles» are about the Earth's magnetism.
fact: A magnetic person is someone people are strongly drawn to.
exercise: The Earth has a «magnetic field» that a compass needle feels. In «magnetic field», what does magnetic mean? | *To do with magnets and magnetism | Hot and bright | Fast and loud | Soft and wet
exercise: Which of these would a magnet pull towards itself? | Wooden spoon | *Iron nail | Plastic cup | Glass jar
exercise: Why are steel cans pulled out by magnets, but aluminium cans are not? | *Steel is magnetic and aluminium is not | Aluminium is heavier than steel | Aluminium is always hot | Steel is always wet
test: A compass needle lines up with the Earth's magnetic field. What is a compass needle? | A bit of glass | *A small magnet free to turn | A piece of wood | A tiny bell
test: «She was a magnetic speaker.» What does magnetic mean about a person? | Made of metal | Easy to lift | Very cold | *People are strongly drawn to her
exam: Which of these is NOT magnetic? | Iron | Steel | Nickel | *Plastic
